{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2595 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6f84a2e3f40177326ba80f9de1c75fe7182ce5468dfc2b37ad5fe29f42ba3a19" :edb/db-before-t 2594 :edb/db-after-t 2595 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535907 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:24:14.757Z" 13194139535907 true] [17592186198302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373859 13194139535907 true] [17592186198304 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373838 13194139535907 true] [17592186198304 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373845 13194139535907 true] [17592186198311 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373852 13194139535907 true] [17592186198507 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535907 true] [17592186198507 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" 13194139535907 true] [17592186198507 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160481.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{6} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n1. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{π}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n2. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n3. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O$." 13194139535907 false] [17592186198507 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373839 13194139535907 true] [17592186373838 1089 "q-160910" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535907 true] [17592186373838 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ad0e3ddbeb228e3fdeeb09823493fb54a4a7f930c25ecd3b69b71dcf77e1d923.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373838 1113 #uuid "36f830e5-a4bd-5767-8e9d-391ce75da407" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373838 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2fbee04fdedee3c2ef0b0f0da2022909ff8d9c2bea7e16b158517afe8fc35c43.png)" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373840 13194139535907 true] [17592186373839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373841 13194139535907 true] [17592186373839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373842 13194139535907 true] [17592186373839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373843 13194139535907 true] [17592186373839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373844 13194139535907 true] [17592186373839 1040 #edb/ref 1759218