edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373844 1111 #uuid "8e3eebcf-491a-5a43-b821-1fa9090c6527" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373846 13194139535907 true] [17592186373845 1089 "q-160928" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535907 true] [17592186373845 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/85e8422a0b3421db73d8cbe88ef3c51b59f628f233d48982840a21c4d26bd26f.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373845 1113 #uuid "0aaf351a-049f-501d-8313-1944db65783b" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373845 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png)" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373847 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373848 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373849 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373850 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373851 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1041 "selection" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373851 13194139535907 true] [17592186373846 1110 #uuid "eef60b9a-278e-5798-b841-d7bb98c5c856" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373847 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/90571cebb5f46697204869a7dbd07e83036d4fe92a5f46045bf6279b3b307999.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373847 1111 #uuid "14f9ff73-afc2-5947-ab98-ae4133fed0df" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373848 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/e718cdc16345d0da40874901ed4dc276c5cf33a75949365b59129a7bb934a8c1.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373848 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373848 1111 #uuid "a6ef46f8-9615-5c8b-96bb-0bf317ed3b86" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373849 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/10a9d70717e657082fd8c36dc98876fcea53297ce79b44585574efef821dba51.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373849 1111 #uuid "36ed1b21-9c88-5a15-a42e-8fa36b4356a6" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373850 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/9d781ed8813991d36d3da4802efa94543f1ee3fc542e1b8c72ff7ad521974e58.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373850 1111 #uuid "db9b7b8f-6de0-50a2-b912-ca7a8a8000e6" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373851 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373851 1111 #uuid "4e935985-d45b-51e1-8d65-92a5a357a0b9" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373852 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373853 13194139535907 true] [17592186373852 1089 "q-161032" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373852 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535907 true] [17592186373852 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373852 1113 #uuid "4fec2c47-e34e-5493-9239-cfee567a5c49" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373852 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373854 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373855 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373856 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373857 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373858 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1041 "selection" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373858 13194139535907 true] [17592186373853 1110 #uuid "c73baaa8-8f23-5b02-ab9f-485244a420b8" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373854 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535907 true] [17592186373854 1111 #uuid "246f6107-5c01-5099-83ed-824fc696247e" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373855 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535907 true] [17592186373855 1111 #uuid "c567f911-29ad-56df-9c93-1830920118bc" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373856 1034 "I only" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535907 true] [17592186373856 1111 #uuid "8f3c907e-9c7c-5d39-948a-d987041dd6fa" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373857 1034 "III only" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535907 true] [17592186373857 1111 #uuid "50ed8644-7bf8-5648-bd64-596232dee69e" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373858 1034 "II only" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535907 true] [17592186373858 1111 #uuid "7296e9f9-a582-5d00-bc5c-f15a143c4dbe" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373859 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373860 13194139535907 true] [17592186373859 1089 "q-161108" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373859 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535907 true] [17592186373859 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/aa8020019d441b741a9b621a0015fda31b3aa199afd8fc145aa840b1749e7a8b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373859 1113 #uuid "a237fc30-2635-5842-8b88-754529781a94" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373859 1145 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png)" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373861 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373862 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373863 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373864 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373865 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1041 "selection" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373861 13194139535907 true] [17592186373860 1110 #uuid "d9cb0a88-4ba9-5308-bc56-858c19707918" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373861 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373861 1111 #uuid "f084828a-15e0-5110-8822-7d37f6c72031" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373862 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/41c26aac58a4c8c2b577f9d3d17c90bd6cbd6971709c42f8a74117db180da899.png" 13194139535907 true] [17592186373862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535907 true] [17592186373862 1111 #uuid "ef333a41-7c55-5af4-8d82-97899f